  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1048 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nScattered thunderstorms from earlier this evening have all but\ndissipated in the absence of diurnal surface heating. Some storms\nmay continue to develop within a zone of ascent ahead of a\nseasonably strong upper low as it passes through the region and\nalong draped outflow over northwest MS. This additional activity\nis expected to remain sub-severe as instability steadily\ndecreases along with bulk shear values below 30 knots. Otherwise,\ncooler temperatures in the low to mid 70s will settle in tonight,\nbut dewpoints in just above 70 F will keep lows out of the 60s.\nHowever, this evening's rains have cooled off much of the region\nand added moisture to the low-levels, enough to where areas of\nfog may develop through Saturday morning depending on how much\nmid-level cloud cover develops. Mid-level water vapor imagery\nfrom this evening shows an area of decent subsidence that should\nkeep clouds from developing from 850 mb upward, which would lean\ntowards a decent fog threat in low-lying areas, particularly\nacross northern MS and West TN.\n\nNo synoptic front will pass through tonight, meaning that much of\nthe surface moisture that was partly responsible for Friday's\nstorms will remain. Additionally, the upper trough may still be\nover portions of northeast MS and West TN through the early\nafternoon hours that would lead to a reinvigoration of\nconvection. Any storms Saturday are expected to be relatively\nisolated and are also not forecast to pose much of a severe\nthreat; mainly gusty winds and locally heavy downpours. Alongside\nthe isolated rain, highs will still manage to reach up into the\nmiddle 90s with heat indices likely climbing above 100 F for the\nmajority of the region.\n\nThe upper trough will have exited the region well in advance of\nSunday morning with the upper ridge coming back to take its place\nas 594+ dm heights make their return. In response, surface\ntemperatures will climb further, making their way into the upper\n90s and perhaps above 100 F Sunday afternoon. Models show that\nmoisture will mix out drastically during the daytime hours,\nleading to heat index values above 100 F, but not above 105 F for\nmost areas, which is our threshold for heat headlines. Some\nlocations throughout the MS River Delta in eastern AR and\nnorthwest MS may still need Heat Advisories into next week.\nRegardless of the presence of a Heat Advisory, conditions will\nstill be humid and hot with little reprieve expected.\nUnfortunately, this forecast is likely to stretch beyond Sunday\nand looks to last through much of next week with similar\nconditions each day. A trend has been emerging within ensembles\nthat point to a gradual, possibly seasonal, weakening of the\nridge by the end of next week, but run-to-run variability remains\nhigh. So, it's probably not best to get our hopes up just yet\nthat the heat will finally retreat.",
